 "result": "Start with these, all in `src/tessel/sessions.py`:\n\n- **`SignedCookieSession` (~line 57–81):** it's a `CallbackDict` that sets `self.modified = True` only through its `on_update` callback, which fires on top-level `__setitem__`, `__delitem__`, `update` and similar calls. `session['cart']['items'].append(x)` mutates a plain nested list/dict that the callback never sees, so `modified` stays `False`. The docstring at lines 60–61 says outright that it \"cannot reliably track\" this.\n- **`SessionInterface.cookie_needs_write` (line 233–245):** it returns `session.modified or (session.permanent and SESSION_REFRESH_EACH_REQUEST)`. With `modified == False` and a non-permanent session, it returns `False`.\n- **`SignedCookieSessionInterface.save_session` (~line 369):** it does `if not self.cookie_needs_write(...): return`, so no `Set-Cookie` is emitted and the nested change is dropped.\n\nThis is documented behavior rather than a regression. The usual fixes are for the user to set `session.modified = True` after nested mutation, or to reassign the top-level key (`session['cart'] = cart`). If you want the library to handle it, you could add a deep-comparison or snapshot check in `cookie_needs_write`, at the cost of extra work on every request.\n\nI only grepped the file, so I haven't read the full bodies of those methods.",
